# A poster for the ferry town's fiesta, read for when, where, how much and what if.
lesson language-the-poster v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=8 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "A poster in the square"
  goal "Read a short notice in [[language.name]] and find the facts in it."

  look {
    scene 32x14 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        passage poster title="[[notice.town-fiesta]]" text="[[notice.fiesta-poster]]" width=30 numbers=true
      }
    }
    say """
      A poster in the square tells everyone about the fiesta. Read it for the facts: which day,
      where, what happens at each time, how much it costs, and what happens if it rains. Words you
      have not met can often be guessed from words you know.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.the-poster-name k=4
    }
    show language.the-poster-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.the-poster-meaning k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show language.the-poster-write k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=5
    show language.the-poster-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=6
    show language.the-poster-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.the-poster-write k=5 easy-k=3 hard-k=4
    show language.the-poster-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.the-poster-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.the-poster-write k=3
    }
  }
  language es {
    try stars=2 {
      show language.es-the-poster-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    }
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.the-poster-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "Read a notice for the facts: which day, where, what time, how much, and what if."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is answering from one word that looks right instead of reading the
    line it is in, and picking the dinner's time for the fireworks. The poster is the same in every
    question, with its lines numbered, so ask "which line says so?" before the child picks. From
    this grade the written words are marked with their accents: a word typed without its accent is
    not taken. The dictation is read from this sheet, where it prints as Read aloud, or played by
    the device. If the child is stuck, read the poster aloud together first, one line at a time. Off
    the page, read a real sign or menu in the language, or make a poster for a family event. It
    meets England's key stage 2 languages programme (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7): "read
    carefully and show understanding of words, phrases and simple writing" and "broaden their
    vocabulary and develop their ability to understand new words that are introduced into familiar
    written material"; Japan's grades 5 and 6 foreign languages: "Reading about familiar and simple
    matters while guessing the meaning of simple words and phrases and basic expressions"; and CEFR
    A2, "Can understand sentences and frequently used expressions related to areas of most immediate
    relevance (e.g. very basic personal and family information, shopping, local geography,
    employment)". The phrases are in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[notice.it-is-free]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[notice.on-saturday]] and [[notice.in-the-square]] and the words to write are [[num.eight]] and [[notice.harbour]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[notice.music-and-dancing]] and [[notice.if-it-rains]] and the words to write are [[notice.august]], [[notice.free]] and [[notice.harbour]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
